{%- comment -%}
  Pincode delivery check. Rendered in the buy box, directly under Add to Cart.

  WHERE THE NUMBERS COME FROM, and what this deliberately does not say.

  This store has no pincode-level shipping data to draw on. There is no
  carrier service connected (carrierServices is empty), so Shopify never
  rates a real courier for a given pincode, and the one domestic delivery
  zone covers all 36 Indian states and union territories with a single flat
  method. Two things follow, and both shape this component:

    Serviceability is universal, not looked up. Every valid Indian pincode
    is inside the delivery zone, so "do you deliver here" has one honest
    answer for the whole country. This does not pretend to query anything.

    The timeframe is the store's own published promise, not a courier
    estimate. dispatch_days and transit_days below are theme settings whose
    defaults match what /pages/support, the shipping accordion and the FAQ
    already tell customers — 1-2 business days to dispatch, 2-5 in transit.
    Change the promise in one place and every surface moves together.

  It says nothing at all about shipping cost, and that omission is
  deliberate rather than an oversight: the storefront promises free shipping
  and the live domestic rate charges Rs 379. Until that is resolved, a
  delivery estimator quoting either figure would be repeating a claim the
  checkout is going to contradict. Delivery time is independent of that
  question, so this answers only the question it can answer honestly.

  The region label is derived from the pincode's first two digits, which is
  a published, stable property of the India Post numbering plan rather than
  a lookup. Where a prefix genuinely spans more than one state the label
  names the group instead of guessing one — a shopper in Raipur reading
  "Chhattisgarh / Madhya Pradesh" learns their area was recognised, which is
  the whole job of that line, without being told something false.

  Business days, not calendar days: the estimate skips Saturdays and Sundays
  because dispatch does. That is why the range is computed in JS from today
  rather than rendered here — Liquid caches, and a date range baked into a
  cached page is wrong by the second day it is served.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign dispatch_min = settings.dispatch_days_min | default: 1 -%}
{%- assign dispatch_max = settings.dispatch_days_max | default: 2 -%}
{%- assign transit_min = settings.transit_days_min | default: 2 -%}
{%- assign transit_max = settings.transit_days_max | default: 5 -%}
<div class="pincode"
     data-pincode
     data-dispatch-min="{{ dispatch_min }}"
     data-dispatch-max="{{ dispatch_max }}"
     data-transit-min="{{ transit_min }}"
     data-transit-max="{{ transit_max }}">
  <label class="pincode-label" for="pincode-input">Check delivery to your pincode</label>
  <div class="pincode-row">
    {%- comment -%}
      type="text" with inputmode="numeric" rather than type="number": a
      number input on a pincode gives a spinner nobody wants, silently
      accepts "1e5", and on some Android keyboards still offers a decimal
      point. maxlength does the constraining instead, and the keypad is what
      inputmode is actually for.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <input type="text"
           id="pincode-input"
           class="pincode-input"
           data-pincode-input
           inputmode="numeric"
           autocomplete="postal-code"
           maxlength="6"
           placeholder="6-digit pincode"
           aria-describedby="pincode-result">
    <button type="button" class="pincode-btn" data-pincode-submit>Check</button>
  </div>
  {%- comment -%}
    role="status" so the answer is announced when it appears. It is not
    role="alert": an invalid pincode is a correction, not an emergency, and
    alert interrupts whatever a screen reader was saying.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <p class="pincode-result" id="pincode-result" data-pincode-result role="status" hidden></p>
</div>
